  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and very humid conditions are expected to \npersist through Tuesday of next week. A Heat Advisory is in \neffect for portions of the area.\n\nA relatively stagnant synoptic pattern is developing across our\nregion. Quasi zonal mid and upper level flow will be in place\nthrough the middle of next week as Bermuda high pressure\npersists offshore. This will keep a steady stream of very warm\nand moist air in place across the region through the period.\n\nA prolonged period of hot and, most notably, very humid \nconditions is expected to remain in place for the next several \ndays. We look to have a weak cold frontal passage early Sunday,\nushering in slightly drier air. This will give some slight \nreprieve from the excessive humidity, though highs will still \nbe in the upper 80s to low 90s. Moisture advection will return \nagain by Monday though, and hot and very humid conditions are \nanticipated to return Monday through Tuesday. A somewhat \nstronger cold front looks to come through late Tuesday or \nWednesday, which will help break the excessive heat and humidity\nsome.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect for portions of eastern \nPennsylvania, much of New Jersey except the far southern \ncounties, as well as New Castle County, Delware. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms through Saturday, as well as much of next week. \nThere is at least a marginal risk each day for localized heavy \nrainfall leading to flash flooding and locally damaging wind \ngusts with any stronger thunderstorms that develop.\n\nDiurnally driven instability will mostly be in the 1000-2000 \nJ/kg each afternoon through the next several days along with \nPWats in the 1.5-2\" range. Mean flow will be predominantly \nsouthwesterly and relatively weak through Saturday. This will \nsupport a continued localized heavy rainfall threat with \npotential for flash flooding, although water loaded downdrafts \ncould cause localized wind damage. Areas with very saturated \nground from recent rains will be most prone to experiencing \ntrees falling.\n\nFor this evening, the main limiting factor is the lack of mid \nlevel forcing and relatively weak low level convergence. As \nsuch, while we will continue to see isolated or even scattered \nshower and storm development, not expecting anything widespread \nor particularly long lived. The main threats will be brief heavy\ndownpours and gusty winds. \n\nOn Friday, a mid level shortwave trough is expected to approach\nlate in the day, providing more forcing (as compared to today).\nAs such, storm coverage is expected to be more widespread, and\nstorms should be more organized although bulk shear values are\nstill relatively modest (up to 25 kt). All that being said,\ncontinued concern for heavy rain/flash flooding and damaging\nwinds. To a lesser extent, there is also some risk for hail,\ngiven the deep unstable layer.\n\nDetails for Saturday's convection remain unclear, but we could\ncertainly end up with some potential for flash flooding and severe\nstorms again. Current indications in the guidance support a\nlater timing of storms during the evening or even overnight\ndepending on the timing of a weak cold front and shortwave\ntrough.\n\nWe look to have a weak cold frontal passage by early Sunday, \nushering in slightly drier air. This combined with subtle \nsubsidence aloft will help briefly limit convection potential on\nSunday and also give some slight reprieve from the excessive\nhumidity, though highs will still be in the upper 80s to low 90s.\nMoisture and forcing aloft will return by Monday as mean flow \nshifts more westerly and mid level flow increases. This should \nresult in a greater severe thunderstorm threat compared to this \nweek, and perhaps slightly lower flash flooding threat, but the\ndetails into the period early next week remain unclear. Long\nstory short, Monday and Tuesday look to continue the hot and \nhumid conditions and daily thunderstorm chances.\n\nA somewhat stronger cold front looks to come through late\nTuesday or Wednesday, which will help break the excessive heat\nand humidity some, but the pattern aloft may still support\ndiurnally driven convection.",
